urname>Schwarze</surname><given-names>FWM</given-names></name><name><surname>Spycher</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Fink</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><year>2008</year><article-title>Superior wood for violins&#x02014;wood decay fungi as a substitute for cold climate.</article-title><source>New Phytologist</source><volume>179(4)</volume><fpage>1095</fpage><lpage>1104</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18554266</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0004245-Stoel1"><label>36</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Stoel</surname><given-names>BC</given-names></name><name><surname>Borman</surname><given-names>TM</given-names></name></person-group><year>2008</year><article-title>A comparison of wood density between classical Cremonese and modern violins.</article-title><source>PLoS ONE</source><volume>3(7)</volume><fpage>e2554</fpage><comment>doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0002554</comment><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18596937</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0004245-Nagyvary6"><label>37</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Nagyvary</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><year>1996</year><article-title>Modern science and the classical violin&#x02014;a view from academia.</article-title><source>The Chemical Intelligencer</source><volume>2(1)</volume><fpage>24</fpage><lpage>31</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0004245-Nagyvary7"><label>38</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Nagyvary</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><year>2005</year><article-title>Investigating the secrets of the Stradivarius.</article-title><source>Education in Chemistry</source><volume>42(4)</volume><fpage>96</fpage><lpage>98</lpage></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn fn-type="conflict"><p><bold>Competing Interests: </bold